"Goblin Tools - Apps on Google Play (https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.goblintools)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.goblintools\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 286\nL0: 【0†Games】【1†Apps】【2†Movies & TV】【3†Books】【4†Kids】\nL1: \nL2: 【0†google_logo Play】\nL3: \nL4: search\nL5: \nL6: help_outline\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Image†fonts.gstatic.com】\nL9: \nL10: 【0†Games】【1†Apps】【2†Movies & TV】【3†Books】【4†Kids】\nL11: \nL12: 【6†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】【6†Image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL13: \nL14: 【6†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL15: # Goblin Tools\nL16: \nL17: 【7†Skyhook Belgium】\nL18: \nL19: In-app purchases\nL20: \nL21: 4.5 star\nL22: \nL23: 1.64K reviews\nL24: \nL25: 【8†Image: Content rating†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL26: \nL27: Everyone\nL28: \nL29: info\nL30: \nL31: 100K+\nL32: \nL33: Downloads\nL34: \nL35: $0.99 Buy\nL36: \nL37: Share\nL38: \nL39: Add to wishlist\nL40: \nL41: 【9†Image: Content rating†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL42: \nL43: Everyone\nL44: \nL45: 【10†Learn more†support.google.com】\nL46: \nL47: 【11†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL48: \nL49: 【12†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL50: \nL51: 【13†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL52: 【14†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL53: \nL54: 【15†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL55: \nL56: 【16†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL57: \nL58: 【17†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL59: \nL60: 【18†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL61: \nL62: 【19†Image: Screenshot image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL63: ## About this app\nL64: \nL65: arrow_forward\nL66: A collection of small, simple tools, for when things feel too big or complicated\nL67: Goblin Tools helps turn overwhelming tasks, confusing messages, difficult decisions, and messy thoughts into manageable next steps.\nL68: This is the official Goblin Tools app associated with goblin.tools. Beware of unofficial copycats. They are not affiliated with Goblin Tools, and some may be misleading or unsafe. Only trust the official app from the verified Goblin Tools publisher.\nL69: Created with neurodivergent people in mind, Goblin Tools is a collection of tiny, clever helpers for everyday life. It can support people with ADHD, autism, executive dysfunction, chronic illness, low-energy days, and anyone who could use a little help getting things done.\nL70: You get:\nL71: - Magic ToDo: Break overwhelming tasks into clear, bite-sized steps\nL72: - Formalizer: Rewrite text to match the tone, style, or level of formality you need\nL73: - Judge: Get a second opinion on the mood, emotion, or intent behind a message\nL74: - Professor: Turn complicated subjects into explanations that are easier to understand\nL75: - Taskmaster: Work through your to-do list one step at a time, without losing focus\nL76: - Consultant: Compare options and get help making a decision\nL77: - Estimator: Create rough time estimates for tasks and activities\nL78: - Compiler: Transform a messy brain dump into an organized list of actions\nL79: - Chef: Invent recipes using the ingredients you already have\nL80: Goblin Tools Pro unlocks features that rely on ongoing online services, storage, and continued development, and therefore cannot be sustainably provided for a single fixed purchase price.\nL81: With Goblin Tools Pro, you can:\nL82: - Keep your data continuously synced between devices\nL83: - Create and reuse templates for to-do items\nL84: - Share the lists you create\nL85: - Make custom Formalizer tones and use your own voice\nL86: - Access your history across all Goblin Tools\nL87: - Use additional Pro features as they are introduced\nL88: Use Goblin Tools for productivity, planning, communication, decision-making, learning, cooking, and getting unstuck when your brain refuses to cooperate.\nL89: It is useful for anyone, but especially designed to help untangle the neurospicy challenges of ADHD, autism, executive dysfunction, spoonie life, and more.\nL90: Purchasing the official app helps keep the Goblin Tools website free and ad-free for everyone, while Goblin Tools Pro supports the ongoing services behind its connected features.\nL91: Smart tools for the neurodivergent\nL92: \nL93: Updated on\nL94: \nL95: Aug 22, 2026\nL96: #2 top paid productivity\nL97: \nL98: Productivity\nL99: ## Data safety\nL100: \nL101: arrow_forward\nL102: \nL103: Safety starts with understanding how developers collect and share your data. Data privacy and security practices may vary based on your use, region, and age. The developer provided this information and may update it over time.\nL104: \nL105:   * 【20†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL106: \nL107: This app may share these data types with third parties\nL108: \nL109: Personal info and App activity\nL110: \nL111:   * 【21†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL112: \nL113: This app may collect these data types\nL114: Personal info\nL115: \nL116:   * 【22†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL117: \nL118: Data is encrypted in transit\nL119: \nL120:   * 【23†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL121: \nL122: You can request that data be deleted\nL123: \nL124: See details\nL125: ## Ratings and reviews\nL126: \nL127: Ratings and reviews are verified info_outline\nL128: \nL129: arrow_forward\nL130: \nL131: Ratings and reviews are verified info_outline\nL132: \nL133: phone_android Phone\nL134: \nL135: tablet_android Tablet\nL136: \nL137: 4.5\nL138: \nL139: 1.6K reviews\nL140: \nL141: 5\nL142: \nL143: 4\nL144: \nL145: 3\nL146: \nL147: 2\nL148: \nL149: 1\nL150: \nL151: 【24†Image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL152: \nL153: Mollie Foerster\nL154: \nL155: more_vert\nL156: \nL157: September 13, 2026\nL158: This app just keeps getting better! I purchased the full version a couple years back (single payment, no subscription!!!) and have been using it asy primary to-do list ever since. Each update has improved QOL. It's still a little tricky to move your items around on the list, but the good outweighs the bad x100. Part of my weekend routine. Keeps me productive, but without me wearing myself out.\nL159: \nL160: 8 people found this review helpful\nL161: \nL162: Did you find this helpful?\nL163: \nL164: Yes\nL165: \nL166: No\nL167: 【25†Image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL168: \nL169: Rowan Noel\nL170: \nL171: more_vert\nL172: \nL173: August 14, 2026\nL174: The update (which honestly made things look worse imo) completely wiped out ALL of my to do lists with no way to get them back. Everything is GONE and honestly the \"easier to navigate\" supposed upgrade just isn't there. Now my keyboard keeps trying to type in the Add new item area instead of before when I add a subtask where instead of a pop-up window (which is a downgrade) it would add it below in a separate line. This isn't a good \"upgrade\"\nL175: \nL176: 6 people found this review helpful\nL177: \nL178: Did you find this helpful?\nL179: Yes\nL180: \nL181: No\nL182: \nL183: Skyhook Belgium\nL184: \nL185: August 14, 2026\nL186: \nL187: Hi there! I'm sorry you're having trouble! If you update to the latest version, there's a new 'migrate' button in the share menu at the bottom of the todo list, that should give you some more info and a retry for recovering your todo list. Let me know if that still goes wrong! Adding subtasks in a separate line is also part of the latest update!\nL188: \nL189: 【26†Image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL190: \nL191: Tori Mercer\nL192: \nL193: more_vert\nL194: \nL195: June 17, 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n‎ADHD Organizer: Llama Life - Ratings & Reviews - App Store (https://apps.apple.com/us/app/adhd-organizer-llama-life/id6454469750?see-all=reviews)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/adhd-organizer-llama-life/id6454469750?see-all=reviews\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 439\nL0: Search [Input]\nL1: \nL2:   * 【0†Today】\nL3:   * 【1†Games】\nL4:   * 【2†Apps】\nL5:   * 【3†Arcade】\nL6: \nL7: Platform\nL8: \nL9:   * 【0†iPhone】\nL10:   * 【4†iPad】\nL11:   * 【5†Mac】\nL12:   * 【6†Vision】\nL13:   * 【7†Watch】\nL14:   * 【8†TV】\nL15: \nL16: # 【9†ADHD Organizer: Llama Life】\nL17: ## Ratings & Reviews\nL18: \nL19:   * 4.5\nL20: \nL21: out of 5\nL22: \nL23: 117 Ratings\nL24: \nL25:   * ### Live. Laugh. Llama. 💕\nL26: \nL27:     1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 02/09/2024\nL28: \nL29: katlady\nL30: For years, there has been a 30/30-shaped hole in my heart, and I have bounced from app to app searching for a worthy replacement. I found the Llama Life website a few weeks ago and was disappointed to see that it was via Internet only, but then I was almost immediately thrilled to read that an iOS app was to release within the month. Talk about perfect timing! Words cannot convey how much this app means to me.\nL31: As an ADHD woman whose sense of time is vestigial at best, finding a app that works with my brain is like birthday and Christmas rolled into one. 30/30 worked for me, and then it just… went away. 😭 I mourned that app the way other people might mourn lost family members. There is no telling how much time and money I’ve wasted searching for a replacement, but the search is finally over! HUZZAH.\nL32: (That’s what the kids say nowadays, right??)To the developers of Llama Life, thank you thank you THANK YOU for all of your hard work. All of the features, the colors, emoji, the ability to break tasks down, to save routines, to easily enter times, the end time estimate, the planned vs. actual time, the divider bar… This app feels like it was designed especially for me. I cannot wait to see where you take it, and I’ll be a subscriber all the way!\nL33: Developer Response\nL34: \nL35: THANK YOU!! Your enthusiasm, support, and honest feedback has made our day! ~ your LL team 🦙\nL36: # Live. Laugh. Llama. 💕\nL37: \nL38:     1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 02/09/2024\nL39: \nL40: katlady\nL41: For years, there has been a 30/30-shaped hole in my heart, and I have bounced from app to app searching for a worthy replacement. I found the Llama Life website a few weeks ago and was disappointed to see that it was via Internet only, but then I was almost immediately thrilled to read that an iOS app was to release within the month. Talk about perfect timing! Words cannot convey how much this app means to me.\nL42: As an ADHD woman whose sense of time is vestigial at best, finding a app that works with my brain is like birthday and Christmas rolled into one. 30/30 worked for me, and then it just… went away. 😭 I mourned that app the way other people might mourn lost family members. There is no telling how much time and money I’ve wasted searching for a replacement, but the search is finally over! HUZZAH.\nL43: (That’s what the kids say nowadays, right??)To the developers of Llama Life, thank you thank you THANK YOU for all of your hard work. All of the features, the colors, emoji, the ability to break tasks down, to save routines, to easily enter times, the end time estimate, the planned vs. actual time, the divider bar… This app feels like it was designed especially for me. I cannot wait to see where you take it, and I’ll be a subscriber all the way!\nL44: Developer Response 02/20/2024\nL45: \nL46: THANK YOU!! Your enthusiasm, support, and honest feedback has made our day! ~ your LL team 🦙\nL47: \nL48:   * ### The only app I use for managing my day!\nL49: \nL50:     1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 02/09/2024\nL51: \nL52: Isabella_ringaling\nL53: I’ve been using Llama Life on desktop for about ten months, and it simplifies and gameifies the dreaded daily to-do list.\nL54: So to now have this handy system on my phone really transforms the entire day for me! I’m able to manage my tasks as I’m doing them in different rooms of the house, or even out and about running errands! As an ADHDer, this is especially helpful as I can get detailed so easily and succumb to distractions and side quests!My favorite part of Llama Life is how they’ve managed to make something like a boring to-do list into something fun! Confetti, chimes, and the colors and themes just make it more appealing.\nL55: I save my preset lists for routine-setting, check over my reports to see if I stuck to my time estimation, and even use their AI-assisted emoji matching to make those tasks even more fun. Can’t wait to see what’s next for Llama Life!\nL56: Developer Response\nL57: \nL58: Thank you so much! Your kind words mean the world to us. It wasn't easy making something boring (the dreaded to-do list) into something fun and whimsical, we're so glad you find it useful and appealing. ~ your LL team 🦙\nL59: # The only app I use for managing my day!\nL60: \nL61:     1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 02/09/2024\nL62: \nL63: Isabella_ringaling\nL64: I’ve been using Llama Life on desktop for about ten months, and it simplifies and gameifies the dreaded daily to-do list.\nL65: So to now have this handy system on my phone really transforms the entire day for me! I’m able to manage my tasks as I’m doing them in different rooms of the house, or even out and about running errands! As an ADHDer, this is especially helpful as I can get detailed so easily and succumb to distractions and side quests!My favorite part of Llama Life is how they’ve managed to make something like a boring to-do list into something fun! Confetti, chimes, and the colors and themes just make it more appealing.\nL66: I save my preset lists for routine-setting, check over my reports to see if I stuck to my time estimation, and even use their AI-assisted emoji matching to make those tasks even more fun. Can’t wait to see what’s next for Llama Life!\nL67: Developer Response 02/20/2024\nL68: \nL69: Thank you so much! Your kind words mean the world to us. It wasn't easy making something boring (the dreaded to-do list) into something fun and whimsical, we're so glad you find it useful and appealing. ~ your LL team 🦙\nL70: \nL71:   * ### The Best!\nL72: \nL73:     1.     2.     3.     4.     5. Aug 8\nL74: \nL75: stayingcrafty\nL76: I love how simple this is to use! You can create as many presets as you want. Like, one morning routine for workdays and one for non workdays. I have a preset for when company is coming because I always need to clean the bathroom, clean the kitchen, etc. So I can quickly load the preset and then add anything I need, like if I'm making food ahead of time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nI have a little over 10 hobbies and end up doing none of them and doom scrolling. Any tips?? : r/Hobbies (https://www.reddit.com/r/Hobbies/comments/1vhefko/i_have_a_little_over_10_hobbies_and_end_up_doing/)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.reddit.com/r/Hobbies/comments/1vhefko/i_have_a_little_over_10_hobbies_and_end_up_doing/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 841\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: Open menu Open navigation Go to Reddit Home\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search Reddit]\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Log In】 Log in to Reddit\nL7: \nL8: Expand user menu Open settings menu\nL9: \nL10: 【2†Go to Hobbies】\nL11: \nL12: 【2†r/Hobbies】\nL13: \nL14: • 2mo ago\nL15: \nL16: 【3†u/Im_Bored--】\nL17: # I have a little over 10 hobbies and end up doing none of them and doom scrolling. Any tips??\nL18: \nL19: Essentially the title. I have a lot of hobbies (Which is kind of growing....), and I love them! But I end up doom scrolling. What was meant to be a quick 5 minutes turns into 4 hours and so on, most of the time even the videos have almost nothing to do with my hobbies. I was wondering if anyone has any tips to focusing on the hobbies more, and managing a lot of hobbies. Thank you for any tips!!!\nL20: (edit) I'll list some of the hobbies I can remember off the top of me head if that helps!\nL21: Sketching/Art in general, 2D animation, Music creation, gym (I want to get into hybrid training with calisthenics as well!), getting into dancing (Specifically Jumpstyle and Shuffle variants), furry related hobbies (Including making a partial fursuit for myself eventually), cooking, bushcraft/outdoors info, gaming, philosophy, singing...and more that I want to learn or want to try out! Hopefully this helps somewhat....\nL22: Edit 2: You guys have such amazing tips!!! I've already started applying them (Set screen time controls on my phone, working on a less crazy schedule, more focused goals, moving my phone to another room, ready-bags for my hobbies to list a few!). Thank you all so much!! I hope other people find useful information in these replies as well if they are in a similar situation! ╰(*°▽°*)╯\nL23: \nL24: 【4†Share】\nL25: \nL26: Sort by:\nL27: \nL28: Best\nL29: \nL30: Open comment sort options\nL31: \nL32: * Best\nL33: \nL34: * Top\nL35: \nL36: * New\nL37: \nL38: * Controversial\nL39: \nL40: * Old\nL41: \nL42: * Q&A\nL43: # Comments Section\nL44: \nL45: 【5†pradaspider】\nL46: \nL47: •\nL48: \nL49: 【6†2mo ago】\nL50: that depends on the specific hobbies you have. as for doom scrolling don’t fall for “quick 5min” thoughts because it’s never just 5 minutes, try habit stacking your hobbies and build them into your day, eg. “every day after lunch i’ll [crochet/play chess/whatever]” or “on mondays after work i’ll [insert hobby]” and put reminders/alarms on your phone. also write your commitment phrases down on sticky notes and put them in a place that makes sense (eg. your dining table)\nL51: \nL52: 【3†Im_Bored--】\nL53: \nL54: •\nL55: \nL56: 【7†2mo ago】\nL57: I'll try these, thank you!\nL58: \nL59: 【8†Arkflow】\nL60: \nL61: •\nL62: \nL63: 【9†2mo ago】\nL64: \nL65: Is this from atomic habits book?\nL66: \nL67: [deleted]\nL68: \nL69: •\nL70: \nL71: 【10†2mo ago】\nL72: \nL73: Comment removed by moderator\nL74: \nL75: 【11†GrandAccomplished444】\nL76: \nL77: •\nL78: \nL79: 【12†2mo ago】\nL80: backing this comment, its important to make it as easy as possible to start hobbies. for example if you'd sketch on a specific table, try to have paper and tools (pencil, etc) ready on the table (in an organized way) or somewhere that is close to the table, so that it isn't a hassle to get them.\nL81: extra points if the paper and tools are visible, as it will remind you every time you pass the supplies that you could sketch, so when you have free time, you're more likey to think \"oh i finally have time to use the supplies that have been staring at me and asking me to use them\".\nL82: It's also important to have hobbies that are dead easy to start doing, eg watching a calm tv show (that you still like) because this gives you a super easy option if you are too tired to do a hobby that requires set up or thinking, but doesnt leave you as tired as scrolling on social media does\nL83: \nL84: 【3†Im_Bored--】\nL85: \nL86: •\nL87: \nL88: 【13†2mo ago】\nL89: \nL90: Balance more mentally intense with more relaxing hobbies is a great idea! Fall back on the more chill hobbies still allows me to do said hobbies! Thank you!\nL91: 【11†GrandAccomplished444】\nL92: \nL93: •\nL94: \nL95: 【14†2mo ago】\nL96: another example of a dead easy hobby is watching youtube videos or consuming content related to your hobby.\nL97: i recommend making a separate account for this and only viewing quality content, i searched for the few quality things i found while on my main account (for many years 💀) so that the algorithm would give me more similar things, as well as asking other people/looking in community resources where only people with authority in the community can post things(as they usually only save good resources)--if you'd like some on art topics i could list some of those places.\nL98: If i see a click bait looking video that i am curious about and want to watch, i save the link and play it on my main profile so my new account's algorithm won't give more of those videos.\nL99: if i want to watch unimportant yt vids i go on my main account 💀 (and yes, i do still use that account)\nL100: \nL101: 【15†more reply】\nL102: \nL103: 【3†Im_Bored--】\nL104: \nL105: •\nL106: \nL107: 【16†2mo ago】\nL108: \nL109: Going to move my phone's charging to another room so it's not right there. And I'll start working on a schedule for myself so I can just focus on a few hobbies per day instead of the pressure of all of them. Making it as easy as possible to do the hobby seems to be a key factor, so I'll get to doing that as well! Thank you!\nL110: \nL111: 【17†suzuukkiii】\nL112: \nL113: •\nL114: 【18†2mo ago】\nL115: \nL116: I put parental time limits on my scrolling apps. The effort it takes to switch them off after my time runs out helps me snap out of the doomscroll. I also put all my hobbies/activities on popsicle sticks and pull from them if I can't decide what to do and feel like scrolling.\nL117: \nL118: 【3†Im_Bored--】\nL119: \nL120: •\nL121: \nL122: 【19†2mo ago】\nL123: \nL124: I didn't think about parental limits! And the popsicle sticks seems fun! Thank you!!!\nL125: \nL126: 【20†dee99s】\nL127: \nL128: •\nL129: \nL130: 【21†2mo ago】\nL131: \nL132: Such a cool idea with the popsicle sticks!\nL133: \nL134: 【22†SlowSundayMaker】\nL135: \nL136: •\nL137: 【23†2mo ago】\nL138: \nL139: Something nobody has said yet: scrolling is not beating your hobbies on enjoyment, it is beating them on start-up cost. Every single thing on your list has a setup tax. Sketching is find the book, find a pencil that is not blunt, find somewhere to sit. Music is open the DAW, load the project, remember where you were. Gym is pack the bag. Scrolling costs zero seconds and it is already in your hand. When you are tired, the cheapest option wins every time, and willpower is not what decides it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlama Life Review: Is the ADHD Task Timer Worth It? - FOCO (https://www.tryfoco.com/llama-life-review/)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.tryfoco.com/llama-life-review/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0:   * 【0†About FOCO】\nL1:   * 【1†ADHD APPS】\nL2:   * 【2†Features】\nL3:   * 【3†Blog】\nL4:   * 【4†Free ADHD Tools】\nL5: 【5†Get the app】\nL6: # Llama Life Review: Is the ADHD Task Timer Worth It?\nL7: \nL8: October 19, 2026 · ADI BEN ELYAHU · 【6†App Reviews & Comparisons】\nL9: \nL10: Updated October 19, 2026 · Prices and ratings checked today · Not tested hands-on, based on official listings · We make FOCO, mentioned in the verdict\nL11: Short answer: Llama Life is a simple ADHD-friendly task timer: you give every task on your list its own countdown, see when the whole list will be done, and get confetti when you finish. It suits people who lose track of time inside a task, and it is paid after a 7-day free trial.\nL12:   * Try Llama Life if you already have a list, and one task quietly eating the afternoon is your real problem.\nL13:   * Skip it if you want a free app, or your list is the problem, not the time.\nL14:   * Pick 【7†FOCO】 if you can’t start the task at all, and you want AI to split it into timed steps with a focus companion on screen.\nL15: ## Llama Life at a glance\nL16: \nL17: Llama Life’s App Store name is “ADHD Organizer: Llama Life,” published by Llama Life Pty Ltd and built by its founder, Marie. Here is what its listings say, checked on October 19, 2026.\nL18: Checked Oct 19, 2026  | 【8†Image: Llama Life app icon】Llama LifePaid after a 7-day trial\nL19: --- | ---\nL20: Best for  | A countdown on every task, one task at a time\nL21: Free version  | No 7-day free trial, then a subscription\nL22: Paid  | US App Store lists $6 a month and $39 a year, plus a $29 six-month plan\nL23: Countdown per task  | Yes The core feature\nL24: List end time  | Yes Total list time and estimated finish\nL25: AI help  | Its founder’s 2024 launch post described AI that breaks big tasks into smaller ones\nL26: Rewards  | Yes Confetti, colors and emoji\nL27: Platforms  | Web, iPhone, Android; one subscription covers all\nL28: Ratings  | ★ 4.5 117 ratings, US App Store. ★ 3.8 Google Play\nL29: ## What does Llama Life look like in use?\nL30: \nL31: The screenshots below are Llama Life’s official App Store screenshots, not our own. We did not test Llama Life hands-on, so we describe what the listing shows and what Llama Life says the app does.\nL32: \nL33: You write your list, and every task gets a time: wake up and get dressed 5 minutes, drink water 1 minute, make breakfast 10 minutes. The top bar adds them up into a total list time and an estimated end time.\nL34: 【9†Image: Llama Life task list with a 5-minute countdown, total list time and end time, from Llama Life's App Store screenshots】Each task gets a countdown, and the top bar shows when the list will be done. Source: Llama Life’s App Store screenshots.\nL35: \nL36: Press play and the current task fills the screen as a countdown, with -5 and +5 buttons when you need more or less time. A visual dial shows the time left as a shrinking wedge of color.\nL37: 【10†Image: Llama Life visual timer dial counting down a Reply to emails task, from Llama Life's App Store screenshots】The visual timer dial on a 40-minute task. Source: Llama Life’s App Store screenshots.\nL38: \nL39: Finishing a task sets off confetti, and you can give each task an emoji or color. Recent updates added soundscapes that keep playing as you switch tabs and stronger alarms when the app is closed.\nL40: ## Is Llama Life built for ADHD?\nL41: \nL42: Mostly, yes. Its App Store name starts with “ADHD Organizer,” and many of the user quotes on its listing come from people with ADHD, though the description itself focuses on focus and timeboxing in general.\nL43: \nL44: The idea behind it is timeboxing: giving a task a fixed amount of time and full attention until the timer ends. Llama Life applies that to every item on a list; one user quoted on its listing calls it “pomodoro on steroids.”\nL45: ## What does Llama Life get right for ADHD brains?\nL46: \nL47: It makes time visible. A countdown for each task, plus a running end time for the whole list, gives an outside clock to people who struggle with time blindness, the trouble sensing how long things take (see our 【11†time blindness guide】).\nL48: \nL49: It keeps attention on one thing. Only the current task is big on screen, which cuts the pull of the other 14 items on the list.\nL50: It rewards finishing. Confetti sounds trivial, but quick, frequent rewards suit how many ADHD brains respond to motivation, which we cover in 【12†ADHD and dopamine】.\nL51: \nL52: It is also more flexible than a fixed 25-minute block. If classic sessions never fit your tasks, our guide to 【13†the Pomodoro method with ADHD】 explains why variable timers can work better.\nL53: ## Where does Llama Life fall short?\nL54: \nL55: It assumes you already know the steps. A countdown helps once a task is clear and sized, but “sort out taxes” with a 60-minute timer can still freeze you before minute one.\nL56: \nL57: That first-minute freeze is task initiation trouble, explained in our guide to 【14†task initiation deficit】. Its founder’s 2024 launch post described AI that breaks big tasks down, but its current store listings don’t describe that feature, so check it in the trial.\nL58: There is no free plan, only a 7-day trial. And its Google Play rating, 3.8, is noticeably lower than its 4.5 on the US App Store, though both are based on fairly few ratings.\nL59: ## How much does Llama Life cost?\nL60: \nL61: Llama Life is free for 7 days, then a subscription. The US App Store lists $6 a month and $39 a year, plus a $4 monthly price point and a $29 six-month plan.\nL62: \nL63: Its founder’s launch post says one subscription covers every platform, so you can use the web app and your phone without paying twice. Check the paywall in the app for your current offer.\nL64: ## Who should try Llama Life?\nL65: \nL66: Try Llama Life if:\nL67: \nL68:   * Your list is fine, but time slips away inside each task.\nL69:   * A visual countdown and a little celebration keep you going.\nL70:   * You want the same list on the web and on your phone.\nL71: \nL72: Look elsewhere if:\nL73: \nL74:   * You want a free tool; see our 【15†Goblin Tools alternatives】.\nL75:   * You want timed steps for repeating routines with a voice guide; see our 【16†Routinery review】.\nL76: \nL77: Pick FOCO if:\nL78:   * You can’t start the task in the first place, and need it broken down first.\nL79:   * You want AI to split it into small timed steps, then a focus companion on screen while you work.\nL80:   * You are fine with a paid app ($59 a year with a 7-day free trial, or $9 a month), on iPhone or Android.\nL81: 【17†Image: FOCO focus mode showing step one of three, 3 minutes to start, with soundscapes and a Start Focus button】FOCO’s focus mode: step one of three, “3 min to start”, with a soundscape and a companion on screen.\n"
